    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Orange is the Orange County seat and the eastern point of the Golden Triangle, on the Sabine River at the Louisiana line, across the Neches River from Jefferson County. Its neighborhoods, businesses, and nearby industrial plants have been hit hard by both rain and wind in recent storms. If your Orange home or business has been damaged, a public adjuster in Orange can help you read your policy, document the loss, and present the claim. Port Arthur Public Adjuster serves Orange, Bridge City, Vidor, and surrounding Orange County communities.</p>

<h2>The big difference: Orange County is not in TWIA</h2>
<p>This is the most important insurance fact for Orange property owners. Jefferson County, home to Port Arthur and Beaumont, is a first-tier coastal county in the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) catastrophe area. <strong>Orange County is not.</strong> According to the <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/wind/generalquestio.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a>, the TWIA area covers specific coastal counties and part of Harris County, and Orange County is not on that list.</p>
<p>What that means in practice:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wind and hail coverage</strong> for an Orange home usually comes from your regular homeowners or commercial insurer, not TWIA. People who cannot find coverage elsewhere may turn to the <a href="https://www.texasfairplan.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas FAIR Plan Association</a>, which offers more limited homeowners coverage.</li>
<li><strong>TWIA's claim rules do not apply.</strong> Your claim follows your policy terms and the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims statute, which generally gives insurers 15 days to acknowledge a claim, 15 business days after receiving requested information to accept or reject it (with possible extensions), and 5 business days to pay after accepting.</li>
<li><strong>TDI windstorm certification (WPI-8) is not required</strong> for TWIA eligibility, though repairs still must meet local codes and permit requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Deductibles still matter.</strong> Many Texas policies have a separate wind/hail or named-storm deductible as a percentage of the dwelling limit. Try the <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Orange storm history</h2>
<h3>Hurricane Harvey (2017)</h3>
<p>According to the <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2017Harvey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Weather Service in Lake Charles</a>, Orange County saw widespread storm totals of 30 to 50 inches of rain from Harvey, and more than 27,742 homes flooded in the county. The city of Orange, Vidor, Orangefield, Pine Forest, West Orange, Mauriceville, Rose City, and Lakeview were listed among the hardest hit. Record crests were seen on Cow Bayou and the lower Neches River, the Sabine River at Orange reached its highest level since Hurricane Ike in 2008, and water covered parts of I-10 near Orange, Vidor, and Rose City.</p>

<h3>Hurricane Laura (2020)</h3>
<p>Laura made landfall near Cameron, Louisiana, on August 27, 2020, as a Category 4 hurricane. The <a href="https://www.weather.gov/lch/2020Laura" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NWS Lake Charles summary</a> reports numerous trees, power lines, and power poles blown down in Orange and Jefferson counties, with some homes and businesses damaged by fallen trees or wind. Hurricane Delta followed on October 9, 2020, near Creole, Louisiana.</p>

<h3>Earlier storms</h3>
<p>Hurricane Rita's 2005 landfall near the Texas&ndash;Louisiana border caused widespread wind damage across Southeast Texas, and Hurricane Ike in 2008 brought wind damage across the region. You can search reports near Orange ZIP codes with the <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a>.</p>

<h2>Orange properties and common claim issues</h2>
<p>Orange has a mix of older homes near downtown and the river, newer subdivisions, and rural properties on larger lots, plus commercial and industrial buildings. Homes are typically slab or pier-and-beam with composition shingle roofs and brick veneer, wood, vinyl, or fiber-cement siding. Common issues we see after storms include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Tree damage.</strong> Laura showed how many trees can come down in one storm. Tree-impact claims raise questions about roof and framing repairs, debris removal limits, and fences.</li>
<li><strong>Flood vs. wind.</strong> Harvey flooding came from rain and rivers, not surge, but it is still flood under most policies. See <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage</a> and <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Mold.</strong> Water left in walls and floors can grow mold quickly in this climate. See <a href="/services/mold-damage-claims/">mold damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Roof disputes.</strong> Insurers sometimes approve only repairs when replacement may be warranted. See <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a>.</li>
</ul>

